Original commit body from e4ff2ac92441760c8e3d496353c9fefb0b070152:

The shadow wrote one JSON file that every run overwrote, so no evidence could
accumulate and the phase's exit criterion could never be evaluated. The
observations are a forward-only time series -- what a commit changed, what the
index knew then, what failed -- so every run without a log was a permanently
lost data point. Now append-only JSONL plus `coverage-select report`.

The criterion is "a miss that ALSO FAILED", and the failing half was never
recorded, so a log alone would have accrued 30 commits of undecidable
evidence. failed_tests_from_junit counts <error> beside <failure>: a
collection error is a test that did not pass, which is the INV-vilag shape.

Raw miss counts are explicitly NOT the criterion. A green run can miss 84
files and lose nothing; counting those rejects the selector on day one.

Three kinds of observation are excluded rather than counted as passes, each
seen live: a cold index, a commit that is all new code, and a collapsed join.
The last was a real ordering defect -- the shadow ran BEFORE the suite and so
read the PREVIOUS run's junit, giving a 0% join and a failure set from another
commit. It now runs after the suite and before the index update, the only
window where this run's results and the pre-run index both exist.

Also fixes a green-run-reported-red abort: fold_selection_index read a bare $1
under `set -u` on the targeted path, aborting after 1,585 tests had passed.

Full suite green: 22,825 passed, 100% coverage. ruff/bandit/mypy clean.

